<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 8 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Adapted from the previous instructor's Lesson 11 written homework:
     Exercise 1 = old Exercise 1, reworked to ask which theorem applies,
     plus new parts (b)-(d) on applying Theorems 8.2 and 8.3.
     Exercise 2 = old Exercise 2, with part (b) asking for a theorem-based
     explanation. Exercise 3 = old Problem 1, reworked: new part (a) (why
     Theorem 8.5 doesn't apply), part (b) uses Theorem 8.1 or 8.2 on the
     pure states, part (c) uses Theorem 8.3 for the mixed states.
     Exercise 4 = old Problem 2 plus a hint for part (c). Exercise 5
     (Rock-Paper-Scissors, no evolutionarily stable state) is new, based
     on book Problem 5.12.4.

     Uses Sections 8.3 and 8.4 (Theorems 8.1, 8.2, 8.3, 8.5) and the idea
     of a symmetric game from Section 7.1.

     Add more by appending another <exercise> below; numbering of the
     existing ones will not shift. -->

<exercises xml:id="ch8-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-ess-unique-symmetric">
    <title>A Completely Mixed Evolutionarily Stable State</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the symmetric two-player game below, with payoffs listed
        as (Player 1, Player 2). Let
        <m>\sigma = \left(\frac{11}{18}, \frac{5}{18}, \frac{2}{18}\right)</m>.
        In an earlier homework, we showed that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a
        mixed strategy Nash equilibrium. Since both players use the same
        mixed strategy in a symmetric game, this is a
        <term>symmetric</term> mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-ess-3x3">
        <title>The game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(2, 2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(6, 3)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(7, 4)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(3, 6)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(3, 3)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(9, 4)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 7)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 9)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-unique-symmetric-only-ne">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The population state <m>\sigma</m> is an evolutionarily stable
          state of the corresponding evolutionary game. (You do not need to
          check this.) Which theorem from Chapter 8 lets you quickly conclude that
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is the only symmetric Nash equilibrium of
          the game? Check that its hypotheses are satisfied. What does the
          same theorem tell you about other evolutionarily stable states?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-unique-symmetric-pure">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Without computing payoffs or using best responses, quickly deduce
          that none of <m>(a, a)</m>, <m>(b, b)</m>, and <m>(c, c)</m> is a
          Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Use a theorem from Chapter 8.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-unique-symmetric-strict">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <em>This part and the next are not about the game above.</em>
          Suppose that in some symmetric game with three pure strategies
          <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>, the profile <m>(a, a)</m> is a
          strict Nash equilibrium. What can you conclude about the population
          state <m>a</m>? What can you conclude about any population state
          in which all three pure strategies are active? Cite the theorems
          you use.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-unique-symmetric-two-mixed">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose a symmetric game has two different symmetric Nash
          equilibria <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> and <m>(\tau, \tau)</m>, and
          all pure strategies are active in both <m>\sigma</m> and
          <m>\tau</m>. Explain why neither <m>\sigma</m> nor <m>\tau</m> is an
          evolutionarily stable state.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Theorem 8.3 is useful here.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <em>Remark:</em> No calculations are needed for this exercise. It
        is about understanding and applying the relevant definitions and
        theorems. One important step in using a theorem is checking that
        its hypotheses are satisfied.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-ess-two-strategies">
    <title>Evolutionary Stability with Two Pure Strategies</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the symmetric two-player game below, where the parameters
        <m>w</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>y</m> satisfy
        <me>0 \lt x \lt y \lt w.</me>
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-ess-wxy">
        <title>The game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(w - x, w - x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(w - y, w)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(w, w - y)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-two-strategies-pure">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all pure strategy Nash equilibria. You can use best responses
          to find and indicate them in the usual way.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-two-strategies-pure-ess">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which of the strategies used in the pure strategy Nash equilibria
          from the previous part (if any) are evolutionarily stable states?
          Explain by citing the relevant theorem(s). No computation is
          needed.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-two-strategies-mixed">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use Theorem 8.5 to find a symmetric mixed strategy Nash
          equilibrium <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>. Is <m>\sigma</m> an
          evolutionarily stable state of the corresponding evolutionary
          game? Explain. You can cite Theorem 8.5 in your explanation. Be
          careful to check its conditions and to say which case you are
          using.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-ess-theorem-8-1">
    <title>Checking the Conditions of Theorem 8.1</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the symmetric two-player game below.
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-ess-4401">
        <title>The game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 4)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 4)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-theorem-8-1-no-8-5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why doesn't Theorem 8.5 apply to this game?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-theorem-8-1-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Notice that <m>(a, a)</m> and <m>(b, b)</m> are Nash equilibria.
          Using the relevant results from Theorem 8.1 or Theorem 8.2,
          determine whether each of the population states <m>a</m> and
          <m>b</m> is evolutionarily stable.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Remark:</em> To satisfy the conditions of Theorem 8.1, they
          must hold for <em>every</em> <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m>. To fail them,
          it is enough to find <em>one</em> <m>\tau</m> where a condition
          fails. Also, be careful to distinguish between a population state
          <m>\sigma</m> and a profile <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> or
          <m>(\sigma, \tau)</m>. For example, it does not make sense to say
          that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is evolutionarily stable.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-ess-theorem-8-1-mixed">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\sigma = (q, 1 - q) = qa + (1 - q)b</m> with
          <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Without computation, use a theorem from
          this chapter to explain why <m>\sigma</m> is not an evolutionarily
          stable state.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Optional:</em> Check this directly by comparing the payoffs of
          <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> against <m>\sigma</m>. You should find that
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is not even a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-owl">
    <title>Hawk, Dove, and Owl</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In earlier assignments, we looked at the hawk-dove game. Here is a
        variation where each player has three strategies. The players are
        fighting over valuable territory, and one of them arrived in the
        territory first. Each player is equally likely to be the one who
        arrived first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each player has three strategies:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>hawk</term> (<m>h</m>): fight aggressively until you win or
            are injured.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>dove</term> (<m>d</m>): bluff by displaying, but retreat if
            the other player begins to fight.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>owl</term> (<m>o</m>): if you arrived in the territory
            first, play <m>h</m> (defend your territory). If the other
            player arrived first, play <m>d</m> (give up the territory if
            they fight for it).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We use the following parameters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>v</m>: the value of the territory.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>w</m>: the cost of getting injured in a fight.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We assume <m>v \lt w</m>: the cost of being injured is greater
            than the benefit of winning the fight.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These assumptions lead to the following payoff matrix, with
        payoffs listed as (Player 1, Player 2).
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-hawk-dove-owl">
        <title>Hawk, dove, and owl</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>o</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v - w}{2}, \frac{v - w}{2}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(v, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{3v - w}{4}, \frac{v - w}{4}\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, v)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v}{2}, \frac{v}{2}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v}{4}, \frac{3v}{4}\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>o</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v - w}{4}, \frac{3v - w}{4}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{3v}{4}, \frac{v}{4}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v}{2}, \frac{v}{2}\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-owl-payoffs">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show how the payoffs for the strategy profile <m>(d, o)</m> were
          calculated.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-owl-pure">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use best responses to find all pure strategy Nash equilibria.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-owl-ess">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each Nash equilibrium from the previous part, determine whether
          the equilibrium strategy is an evolutionarily stable state of the
          corresponding evolutionary game.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For each Nash equilibrium, consider:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Is it symmetric, so that both players use the same strategy?
              Only a symmetric Nash equilibrium <m>(s, s)</m> can give an
              evolutionarily stable state <m>s</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If it is symmetric, is it a strict Nash equilibrium? If so,
              what does that tell you about whether <m>s</m> is
              evolutionarily stable?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </hint>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-rps-no-ess">
    <title>Rock-Paper-Scissors Has No Evolutionarily Stable State</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In Rock-Paper-Scissors (Problem 5.12.4 in the book), paper beats
        rock, scissors beats paper, and rock beats scissors. The winner gets
        <m>1</m>, the loser gets <m>-1</m>, and a tie gives both players
        <m>0</m>. Payoffs are listed as (Player 1, Player 2).
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-rps-no-ess">
        <title>Rock-Paper-Scissors</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>r</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>p</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>s</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>r</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-1, 1)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, -1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>p</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, -1)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-1, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>s</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-1, 1)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, -1)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>\sigma = \left(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{3}\right)</m>,
        the strategy that plays rock, paper, and scissors equally often.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-rps-no-ess-nash">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Compute the payoff of each pure strategy <m>r</m>, <m>p</m>, and
          <m>s</m> against <m>\sigma</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-rps-no-ess-not-stable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use Theorem 8.1 to show that <m>\sigma</m> is not an
          evolutionarily stable state.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          It is enough to find <em>one</em> <m>\tau \neq \sigma</m> for which
          a condition of Theorem 8.1 fails.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-rps-no-ess-none">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You may use the fact (Problem 5.12.4 in the book) that
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is the only Nash equilibrium of
          Rock-Paper-Scissors. Using a relevant theorem from this chapter,
          explain why the corresponding evolutionary game has <em>no</em>
          evolutionarily stable state at all.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <em>Remark:</em> By Theorem 7.1, every symmetric game has a
        symmetric Nash equilibrium. This exercise shows that nothing
        similar is true for evolutionarily stable states: being
        evolutionarily stable is a stronger requirement than being a Nash
        equilibrium, and some games have no evolutionarily stable state.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

</exercises>
